      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m confused about what exactly this achieves: it looks like it\u0027s just converting RSMRST from being treated as active-high to active-low, but that seems like it would require changes to every user so the inputs are inverted again. Looking at Nissa, `RSMRST_PWRGD_L` is active-low (so it\u0027s in reset when the GPIO is low) but the input isn\u0027t inverted in configuration so this would break boot on Nissa.\n\nIn principle I think it\u0027s a good idea to treat the power signal as active-high because its name indicates it\u0027s active-high, but changing the interpretation looks like it needs to invert the inputs for every board that configures this signal to avoid breaking them.",
      "revId": "c12d424e59f66caf31e7002b9a0dee7274a5e32f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f2e36cad_a7ecb477",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-04T17:10:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The AP input/EC output RSMRST# is active low. So my expectation was that I would use the `GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW` flag on this pin.  This would match what is done for the SLP_Sx# inputs, which all set `GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW`.\n\nYou\u0027re correct that this change requires modifications to existing boards, but I think it\u0027s clearer to be consistent with the active low flag across all the power signals.",
      "parentUuid": "e7c7f20e_afd4c979",
      "revId": "c12d424e59f66caf31e7002b9a0dee7274a5e32f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
